Subject: the default layout belongs to the desktop, not to a look

The desktop a new account starts with -- icons on the desktop, the bar at
the bottom with Start, the open apps, the tray and the clock -- is built
by a script. libplasma looks for it in two places, in this order: the
active look-and-feel package's layouts/<shell>-layout.js, then this shell
package's contents/layout.js, which its Plasma/Shell structure maps to
the key `defaultlayout`.

Raye's was in the look-and-feel package. So the shape of the desktop was
part of the theme: it travelled with the colours, and a look that carries
no layout script -- or any look but Raye's -- handed the account somebody
else's desktop. It is now this package's own, where it belongs: changing
the colour scheme, or Night for Day, leaves the layout alone.

The layout script's own test comes with it, in the Raye test directory
patch 0001 set up, against a stand-in of the scripting API: that it runs,
that the taskbar is built and keeps its widgets, that an account without
the Raye applets still gets a usable bar rather than none, and that no
containment action is written under a containment id.

⚠️ The Raye look-and-feel package must stop shipping layouts/ in the same
release, because it is read first; and it may not stop shipping it before
this package is installed, or a new account would be handed no layout at
all and start without a taskbar. The image build refuses that combination
rather than make such an image (raye/iso, DESKTOP_DEBS).

🚨 And until now that was the *only* thing refusing it, at image build and
nowhere else: `apt install raye-theme` onto an upstream plasma-desktop gave
a first login with no taskbar at all, because the Raye look ships no
layouts/ any more and upstream's shell package has never had this
layout.js. So the package that ships it now declares
`Provides: raye-desktop-layout`, and raye-theme depends on that name.

⚠️ The name is a virtual package and not `Depends: plasma-desktop-data`,
because Ubuntu's own build of plasma-desktop-data satisfies that package
name and carries no Raye layout: the dependency would go green against
precisely the package it exists to exclude. Nor can it be versioned around
-- the version here is <ubuntu>+raye<n>, which no Depends can name. And the
Provides is deliberately unversioned: the property wanted is "a Raye layout
is installed", and pinning +raye<n> would break the dependency on every
Ubuntu desktop update until everything naming it is rebuilt too.

⚠️ What it does on an Ubuntu desktop update is the point, and worth stating
plainly rather than overselling: when apt takes Ubuntu's newer
plasma-desktop-data, the virtual package goes with it, so raye-theme's
dependency can no longer be met. apt then either holds the update back or
offers to remove raye-theme -- it has to *say* something, where today the
replacement is silent. Removing raye-theme is not the taskbar-less state:
without /etc/xdg/kdeglobals the active look is Breeze again, and Breeze's
own layout applies. The state this forbids is the other one, raye-theme
kept on a desktop package that has no layout.

🚨 Measured, and it decides which package carries the Provides:
debian/plasma-desktop-data.install is the only one here taking
usr/share/plasma, and plasma_install_package(desktoppackage ... shells)
installs under it. Breeze's layouts/, the one that was being read instead,
belongs to plasma-workspace-data -- a different source package, which is
why the lookup itself is corrected in patches/plasma-workspace/0001.

Reason: the property wanted here cannot be had outside the source. A
layout in a look-and-feel package is, by construction, part of a look;
the only file libplasma reads for a layout that belongs to the desktop
itself is this package's contents/layout.js, and this package owns that
path -- shipping a second copy of it from another package is a file
conflict, not a configuration. The same holds for its test, which has to
run where the script is built.

